window.EXAM_DATA = {
  "id": "exam1",
  "title": "Exam 1 — Week 1 Comprehensive Assessment (70 Questions)",
  "questions": [
    {"stem": "A 72-year-old on furosemide 40 mg daily for heart failure reports weakness and palpitations. Labs show K+ 3.1 mEq/L, Cr 1.8 mg/dL. The patient denies polyuria but admits to occasional dizziness on standing. Which interventions require immediate provider notification?", "options": ["Check ECG for peaked T waves; increase K+ supplementation immediately", "Obtain orthostatic vitals and ECG; discuss K+ repletion and potential dose adjustment", "Increase fluid intake and add salt to diet to counteract diuretic effect", "Reassure patient that hypokalemia is expected and will resolve in a week"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Hypokalemia (3.1) with cardiac symptoms + elevated Cr suggest both K+ depletion and possible renal impairment from diuretic overdrive. Peaked T waves appear with hyperkalemia (not hypokalemia). Orthostatic vitals assess dehydration. ECG confirms arrhythmia risk. The provider must adjust the diuretic dose and prescribe K+ repletion—this is not routine and requires immediate attention. Adding salt/fluids contradicts heart failure treatment.", "topic": "Diuretic/Antihypertensive Safety", "section": "diuretics-antihypertensives", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient taking an ACE inhibitor for hypertension develops a dry cough lasting 3 weeks and reports mild throat tightness 'like a tickle.' Blood pressure is well-controlled. Which action is most appropriate?", "options": ["Continue the ACE inhibitor; the cough is a benign side effect that will resolve", "Contact the provider—throat tightness may signal angioedema onset, a contraindication", "Switch to a beta-blocker without waiting for provider approval to manage the cough", "Obtain a throat culture to rule out infection before considering medication changes"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Dry cough is common with ACE inhibitors (bradykinin effect), but throat tightness is a red flag for angioedema—a potentially life-threatening airway emergency. Even though the sensation is mild now, angioedema can progress rapidly. Provider notification is mandatory. Do not continue the ACE inhibitor if angioedema is suspected. Do not self-switch medications. Throat culture is not indicated.", "topic": "Diuretic/Antihypertensive Safety", "section": "diuretics-antihypertensives", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A 65-year-old on a beta-blocker for post-MI management wakes at 3 AM feeling shaky and sweaty, with a fingerstick glucose of 48 mg/dL. The patient denies typical hypoglycemic symptoms like palpitations or tremor 'because I usually feel that.' Which best explains this presentation?", "options": ["The patient has developed brittle diabetes and requires insulin adjustment immediately", "Beta-blockers mask adrenergic hypoglycemia symptoms (tremor, palpitations), reducing awareness", "Nocturnal hypoglycemia is unrelated to beta-blocker use and indicates poor diabetes control", "The patient's age and post-MI status make hypoglycemia symptoms less reliable"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Beta-blockers block the sympathetic response to low glucose, masking tremor, palpitations, and anxiety—the classic hypoglycemia warning signs. The patient may only recognize confusion, sweating, or shakiness. This 'hypoglycemia unawareness' is a known beta-blocker side effect and increases hypoglycemic emergency risk. Age and MI history don't explain symptom masking; the drug does. Patient education about alternate warning signs is critical.", "topic": "Diuretic/Antihypertensive Safety", "section": "diuretics-antihypertensives", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient on a thiazide diuretic for hypertension has orthostatic vital signs: lying BP 142/88 (HR 72), standing 1 min BP 118/70 (HR 88), standing 3 min BP 116/68 (HR 90). The patient denies dizziness. Which is the most accurate interpretation?", "options": ["Orthostatic hypotension is present and symptomatic; fall precautions are mandatory", "Orthostatic hypotension is present but asymptomatic; consider dose reduction or fluid increase", "Blood pressure is well-controlled; no orthostatic hypotension based on these readings", "The HR rise alone (72 to 90) indicates compensation and requires medication adjustment"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Orthostatic hypotension = drop in systolic ≥20 mmHg (142 to 118 = 24 mmHg drop) or diastolic ≥10 mmHg (88 to 70 = 18 mmHg drop) or symptoms. This patient meets criteria despite denying dizziness—symptoms may develop with further ambulation or in certain positions. The HR rise (72 to 90) shows compensation. Asymptomatic orthostatic hypotension still warrants intervention: reduce dose, increase fluids, or both. Fall risk exists even without current symptoms.", "topic": "Diuretic/Antihypertensive Safety", "section": "diuretics-antihypertensives", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A 58-year-old on an ACE inhibitor for heart failure develops fatigue, anorexia, and weakness. Labs: K+ 6.8 mEq/L, Cr 2.1 mg/dL (baseline 1.0). Which mechanism best explains the lab findings, and what is the priority action?", "options": ["Dehydration from excessive diuretic use; increase free water intake and reassess in 2 days", "Hyperkalemia from ACE inhibitor + renal decline; hold ACE inhibitor and notify provider immediately", "Expected side effect of ACE inhibitor therapy; monitor weekly and continue current dose", "Hyperglycemia causing osmotic diuresis; check glucose and adjust insulin if diabetic"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "ACE inhibitors block aldosterone, reducing urinary potassium excretion. In declining renal function (Cr 2.1, double baseline), K+ accumulates rapidly, causing hyperkalemia (6.8). Symptoms—fatigue, anorexia, weakness—are consistent with hyperkalemia. This is a medical emergency (life-threatening arrhythmias can occur). Hold the ACE inhibitor immediately and notify the provider. Do not wait for follow-up. This is not a minor or expected side effect.", "topic": "Diuretic/Antihypertensive Safety", "section": "diuretics-antihypertensives", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A 42-year-old on insulin presents to the ED with tremor, confusion, and diaphoresis. Point-of-care glucose is 55 mg/dL. Vital signs: BP 148/92, HR 112, RR 20. Which sequence of actions is most appropriate?", "options": ["Immediately administer 25 mL of 50% dextrose IV and recheck glucose in 15 minutes", "Give 15 grams of rapid-acting carbohydrate (juice, glucose tablet) orally and recheck in 15 minutes", "Call 911 first, then establish IV access for dextrose administration", "Lay the patient flat, check for signs of stroke, and obtain a stat CT head"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Symptomatic hypoglycemia (tremor, confusion, diaphoresis at glucose 55) is a medical emergency. First-line: 15 g rapid carbs PO (juice, glucose tablet). Recheck in 15 min; if still low, repeat. IV dextrose is reserved for unconscious/unable-to-take-PO patients. Do not assume stroke (hypoglycemia mimics neurologic symptoms). Lay flat prevents falls but is secondary to carb administration. The patient is conscious and can swallow, so oral route is fastest.", "topic": "Insulin Safety", "section": "insulins", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "Before administering rapid-acting insulin to a patient, the nurse checks glucose and finds 68 mg/dL. The provider order is 'insulin aspart 10 units before meals.' Which action is correct?", "options": ["Administer the insulin as ordered to prevent hyperglycemia at mealtime", "Hold the insulin and notify the provider; recheck glucose in 15 minutes", "Give 50% of the dose (5 units) as a compromise to prevent hypoglycemia", "Administer the insulin and monitor glucose closely for 2 hours post-injection"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Pre-meal glucose of 68 is low-normal to borderline. Rapid-acting insulin in this state risks hypoglycemia (60–70 is approaching danger). Always verify current glucose before injecting—this is a critical safety check. Do not modify doses on your own; notify the provider. Some protocols allow proceeding if glucose >70–80, but this patient's trend and timing matter. A 50% dose is never a solo nursing decision without provider order. Monitoring post-injection does not prevent the hypoglycemia that injection may cause.", "topic": "Insulin Safety", "section": "insulins", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "An 81-year-old patient with type 2 diabetes becomes acutely confused and combative. The patient's daughter reports the confusion started 2 hours ago and denies recent falls. Glucose check: 342 mg/dL. Vital signs: BP 156/94, HR 98, RR 24, temp 97.8°F. Which interpretation is most accurate?", "options": ["Acute confusion is a sign of diabetic ketoacidosis; give insulin stat and draw arterial blood gas", "The patient likely has hypoglycemia despite the high glucose reading; recheck with a different meter", "Hyperglycemia can cause altered mental status, but checking for concurrent hypoglycemia and infection is important", "Confusion in the elderly is normal and unrelated to glucose; perform a neuro check and order CT head"], "answer": 2, "rationale": "Glucose 342 is not immediately life-threatening on its own, but in older adults, even mild hyperglycemia + dehydration + infection can cause acute confusion (hyperosmolar state). However, rule out hypoglycemia (meter error, meter malfunction), infection (UTI is silent in elderly), and stroke. Atypical illness in older adults: confusion instead of fever, chest pain, or dysuria. Perform comprehensive assessment before attributing confusion to hyperglycemia alone. DKA is rare in type 2 and typically presents with metabolic acidosis (labs needed). Do not give insulin stat without context.", "topic": "Insulin Safety", "section": "insulins", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient on insulin takes the injection at 6 PM as usual but then delays dinner by 2 hours due to a work meeting. By 8 PM, the patient is hungry but feels shaky. Glucose is 62 mg/dL. Which patient teaching point would prevent this recurrence?", "options": ["Always inject insulin immediately before eating, not before; timing must match food intake exactly", "If dinner is delayed, take a longer-acting insulin instead to provide more stable coverage", "Eat a small carbohydrate snack (10–15 g) if meals are delayed beyond the insulin onset time", "Reduce the insulin dose by half on days when meals are unpredictable to prevent hypoglycemia"], "answer": 2, "rationale": "Rapid-acting insulins peak 1–2 hours post-injection. Injecting at 6 PM for a 8 PM meal creates a 2-hour gap—by 8 PM, insulin is peaking with no food on board, causing hypoglycemia. Teaching: if a meal will be delayed, take a small snack (10–15 g carbs) during the insulin peak. Do not tell patient to delay injection (they can't predict work emergencies). Do not change insulin type or dose without provider order. Timing is key: coordinate injection with when food is ready, not clock time.", "topic": "Insulin Safety", "section": "insulins", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A 55-year-old on insulin reports consistent fasting glucose 180–210 mg/dL despite 'taking the injection every night at 9 PM as prescribed.' The provider suspects basal insulin is insufficient. Before adjusting the dose, which question is most important to ask?", "options": ["Are you eating too many carbohydrates at dinner, causing overnight hyperglycemia?", "Which insulin type are you using, and are you storing it at the correct temperature?", "Have your work hours changed, causing stress-induced hyperglycemia overnight?", "Are you using a meter that is recalibrated regularly, to ensure glucose readings are accurate?"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Before increasing a dose, verify the insulin type, storage, and expiration. Expired or improperly stored insulin loses potency. NPH (intermediate-acting) and long-acting insulins may be in use; storage at room temp (>86°F) or repeated freeze-thaw ruins efficacy. If insulin is compromised, increasing the dose won't help. Also verify meter calibration. Carb intake, stress, and sleep changes can affect fasting glucose, but dose adjustment is premature without ruling out insulin quality/storage. This one question prevents unnecessary dose escalation.", "topic": "Insulin Safety", "section": "insulins", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A 68-year-old taking ibuprofen 800 mg three times daily for arthritis pain presents with fatigue and a hemoglobin of 9.2 g/dL (baseline 13.5). Stool is dark. BUN is 28, Cr 1.7 (baseline 1.0). Which nursing actions are appropriate?", "options": ["Give iron supplementation immediately to address anemia; recheck Hgb in 2 weeks", "Hold the NSAID, notify provider, check reticulocyte count/iron studies, assess for bleeding", "Switch to acetaminophen without provider order since it is safer than NSAIDs", "Recommend increasing dietary protein and calcium to rebuild blood counts faster"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Dark stool (melena) + anemia + elevated BUN/Cr suggests GI bleeding from chronic NSAID use. NSAIDs cause gastric ulceration and can precipitate AKI. Hold the drug immediately. Notify provider. Obtain reticulocyte count (indicates bone marrow response), iron studies, and possibly GI consultation. Do not give iron without investigating the source (iron won't stop bleeding). Do not switch to acetaminophen without provider order (dosing concerns with renal function). Nutritional support is secondary to stopping the bleeding.", "topic": "NSAID/Steroid Safety", "section": "nsaids-steroids", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A 71-year-old on an NSAID for chronic pain and a diabetic on metformin presents with nausea, fatigue, and decreased urine output. SCr has risen from 1.1 to 2.2 mg/dL in 2 weeks. The patient reports 'trying to stay dehydrated to reduce pain swelling.' Which is the priority intervention?", "options": ["Encourage fluid intake immediately and hold metformin until renal function stabilizes", "Increase the NSAID dose to achieve better pain control and improve quality of life", "Start aggressive IV hydration without assessment to reverse the AKI rapidly", "Perform orthostatic vitals, notify provider, and begin IV fluid replacement cautiously"], "answer": 3, "rationale": "NSAIDs reduce renal perfusion; dehydration worsens this. Combined with metformin (contraindicated in renal failure—lactic acidosis risk), this patient has acute kidney injury (AKI). Metformin must be held. However, aggressive IV hydration without assessing volume status can cause fluid overload (especially in cardiac/renal patients). Check orthostatics first. Notify provider for cautious fluid replacement and labs (electrolytes, lactate if metformin was taken). Pain control is secondary to preventing ESRD. Do not increase NSAID in renal failure.", "topic": "NSAID/Steroid Safety", "section": "nsaids-steroids", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient on low-dose prednisone for an autoimmune condition now has persistent high glucose readings (fasting 160–180 mg/dL, previously 95–110). HbA1c is 7.2% (previous 5.8%). Which explanation and action are most appropriate?", "options": ["The patient likely has developed type 2 diabetes; start metformin and increase prednisone for disease control", "Steroids increase hepatic glucose production and decrease insulin sensitivity; notify provider for glucose management", "This is temporary hyperglycemia from stress; recheck glucose in a week without intervention", "Recommend dietary changes and exercise; increase insulin if diabetic, but continue prednisone unchanged"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Steroids are diabetogenic: they increase hepatic glucose production, decrease insulin secretion, and impair insulin sensitivity. Fasting glucose rise + elevated HbA1c confirm steroid-induced hyperglycemia. This is not transient stress-related glucose elevation. Notify the provider: may need to taper prednisone (if possible), start glucose-lowering therapy, or adjust existing diabetes medications. Do NOT increase prednisone to 'improve disease control'—this worsens hyperglycemia. Dietary changes alone won't overcome steroid-induced hyperglycemia. Monitor glucose closely.", "topic": "NSAID/Steroid Safety", "section": "nsaids-steroids", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A 62-year-old on systemic corticosteroids for 6 months develops severe back pain and is found to have a vertebral compression fracture on X-ray. BMD scan shows significant osteoporosis (T-score -3.2). Which nursing intervention is most important going forward?", "options": ["Recommend high-impact exercise (running, jumping) to rebuild bone density quickly", "Educate on calcium/vitamin D supplementation, weight-bearing activity, and the need to discuss steroid-sparing options with the provider", "Order a stat physical therapy consult to initiate aggressive weight-bearing exercises immediately", "Reassure the patient that steroid-induced osteoporosis is temporary and will resolve after stopping steroids"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Long-term steroids cause bone loss through multiple mechanisms (reduced osteoblast activity, calcium malabsorption, increased osteoclast activity). By 6 months, significant osteoporosis and fracture risk are established. Teaching: calcium 1000–1200 mg/day + vitamin D 800–2000 IU/day, weight-bearing activity (walking, not running initially—fracture risk), and discussion with provider about tapering steroids or switching to a steroid-sparing agent. High-impact exercise is contraindicated with acute fracture. Recovery from steroid-induced osteoporosis is slow and incomplete. Prevention is key.", "topic": "NSAID/Steroid Safety", "section": "nsaids-steroids", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient on chronic corticosteroids presents with a productive cough, fever (101.2°F), and fatigue. WBC is 7.2 (normal range), and chest X-ray shows bilateral infiltrates. Which clinical interpretation is most accurate?", "options": ["Normal WBC rules out infection; the infiltrates are likely from steroid-induced side effects", "Steroid-induced immunosuppression may mask typical infection signs; infection is still likely and warrants treatment", "The fever is expected with corticosteroid therapy; monitor for 48 hours before starting antibiotics", "Bilateral infiltrates indicate pulmonary edema from steroid-induced hypertension; diuretics are first-line"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Steroids suppress immune response, reducing fever and WBC elevation even in serious infections. A 'normal' WBC does NOT rule out infection in an immunosuppressed patient. Fever (101.2°F) + productive cough + bilateral infiltrates = pneumonia until proven otherwise. Treat empirically (sputum culture, antibiotics) rather than waiting. Steroid-induced side effects don't cause infiltrates. Fever is not 'expected' with steroids and warrants investigation. Pulmonary edema typically shows unilateral or perihilar infiltrates with elevated BNP. Clinical judgment: maintain high suspicion for infection in immunosuppressed patients despite 'normal' WBC.", "topic": "NSAID/Steroid Safety", "section": "nsaids-steroids", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "During morning rounds, a post-op patient with COPD is alert but confused, claiming 'the room is spinning.' Vital signs: BP 128/82, HR 108, RR 28, O2 sat 88% on 2L nasal cannula (patient's chronic goal is 88–92%). Temperature 37.2°C. Which is the priority assessment?", "options": ["Check for vertigo triggers (inner ear dysfunction, medication side effects) and position the patient supine", "Assess airway, breathing, and oxygen saturation; ensure adequate oxygenation and consider hypercarbia effects", "Order a STAT CT head to rule out stroke or intracranial pathology", "Obtain an ECG to rule out cardiac arrhythmia as the cause of confusion"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Post-op COPD patient at goal O2 sat 88% with RR 28 and confusion = possible hypercapnia (CO2 retention). In COPD, high O2 can suppress respiratory drive, worsening CO2 retention and causing narcosis (confusion, dizziness). ABCs first: check blood gas, assess work of breathing, monitor for accessory muscle use. Hypercarbia causes confusion and can mimic vertigo/stroke. Vertigo workup is premature; this is a respiratory/metabolic issue. CT and ECG may be needed after addressing hypoxia/hypercapnia. Lower O2 delivery cautiously if CO2 is high.", "topic": "Assessment Priority", "section": "assessment", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A 75-year-old hospitalized with pneumonia suddenly becomes acutely confused and combative. Temperature 38.1°C, BP 142/90, HR 102, RR 26, O2 sat 94% on 3L nasal cannula. Glucose 118 mg/dL. The patient has no prior cognitive impairment. Which assessment question is most critical?", "options": ["Does the patient have a history of dementia or prior episodes of delirium?", "Has the patient received any sedating medications, pain medications, or anticholinergics in the past 24 hours?", "Is the patient's oxygen saturation truly adequate, or is this confusion from hypoxemia despite the reading?", "Has the patient had a urinalysis and urine culture to rule out UTI-induced delirium?"], "answer": 2, "rationale": "New-onset acute confusion in an older adult is a medical emergency until proven otherwise. Atypical presentation of illness in elderly: confusion instead of fever, chest pain, or dysuria. While UTI, medications, and history matter, the most immediate assessment is oxygenation adequacy. Pulse ox 94% can be deceptive—sensor placement, movement, or hypercapnia can skew readings. Check blood gas for both O2 and CO2. Check O2 sat on both sides of body (equipment failure?). With pneumonia, RR 26, and acute confusion, assume hypoxemia/hypercarbia until proven otherwise. Address oxygenation, then investigate other causes (meds, infection, metabolic).", "topic": "Assessment Priority", "section": "assessment", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A 58-year-old admitted for heart failure exacerbation is on IV furosemide, oxygen, and medications. Over 6 hours, urine output drops from 200 mL/hr to 20 mL/hr. BP drops from 124/82 to 98/60. Patient is alert but reports mild dizziness on standing. Which comprehensive assessment should the nurse perform?", "options": ["Assess neck veins, lung sounds, peripheral edema, and skin turgor to determine volume status", "Check orthostatic vitals, assess skin perfusion, measure CVP if available, and review recent intake/output", "Obtain immediate renal ultrasound to rule out acute kidney injury", "Increase IV fluid rate to restore urine output and blood pressure back to baseline"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Diuretic-induced oliguria + hypotension + dizziness = over-diuresis (dehydration) vs. cardiogenic shock. Comprehensive assessment: JVD (absent = dehydration, present = pulmonary edema still present), lung auscultation (crackles = fluid overload, clear = over-diuresed), peripheral edema (loss = over-diuresed), skin turgor and mucous membranes (dry = dehydration). Orthostatic vitals assess perfusion. CVP (if available) distinguishes low filling pressure (dehydration) from high (failure). Labs: BUN/Cr ratio (BUN >> Cr suggests dehydration), electrolytes. Do not blindly increase fluids—may worsen heart failure. Assessment guides intervention.", "topic": "Assessment Priority", "section": "assessment", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient recovering from abdominal surgery has a Foley catheter, NG tube to suction, two peripheral IVs, and a drain at the surgical site. During morning assessment, which findings require provider notification?", "options": ["NG tube draining green/brown secretions; Foley output dark yellow; drain output serosanguineous", "Foley output tea-colored; NG tube output bilious; drain output bright red and increasing", "NG tube draining tan liquid; Foley pale yellow; drain with minimal serosanguineous output", "All of the above require notification; any drainage change warrants provider review"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Every tube and line is part of ongoing assessment. Normal findings: NG green/brown (bile/GI content), Foley pale yellow (dilute urine), drain serosanguineous (serous + minimal blood). Concern signs: Foley tea-colored (concentrated—dehydration or myoglobinuria), NG bilious (possible obstruction), drain bright red increasing (excessive bleeding). Do not ignore 'minor' changes. Option 3 is normal—no notification needed. Option 1 is normal. Option 2 = problem: investigate dehydration (tea-colored urine), possible NG obstruction, and excessive bleeding. The correct answer requires notification.", "topic": "Assessment Priority", "section": "assessment", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "In clinical, your patient has a tracheostomy on a ventilator with an ET tube marker at 22 cm at the teeth. Over your 12-hour shift, you notice the mark has moved to 25 cm (tube appears to have migrated deeper). Breath sounds are diminished on the left. Which is the priority action?", "options": ["Document the finding and report to the day nurse at shift change; it is likely inconsequential", "Notify the provider immediately; secure the tube at its current position; assess for right mainstem intubation", "Retape the tube at the 22 cm mark yourself to restore its original position", "Suction the tube and reassess breath sounds; tube migration is normal with patient movement"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Tube migration deeper (22 to 25 cm) risks right mainstem intubation, causing left lung collapse and hypoxemia. Diminished left breath sounds confirm this. This is NOT normal and NOT inconsequential—it can cause respiratory compromise. Notify provider immediately. Secure the tube at its current position (do not retape it higher without provider order—could kink or damage the tube). Obtain a chest X-ray to confirm tube placement. Suction does not address the position problem. This requires urgent intervention, not handoff at shift change.", "topic": "Assessment Priority", "section": "assessment", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "You are suctioning an orally intubated patient's endotracheal tube. After 8 seconds of suctioning, the patient's oxygen saturation drops from 98% to 91%, and the heart rate rises to 118. Which action is appropriate?", "options": ["Continue suctioning to completion; the desaturation will recover quickly after the pass", "Stop suctioning immediately; allow the patient to recover and reoxygenate before resuming", "Increase the oxygen delivery to 100% before the next suctioning pass to prevent further drops", "Switch to a smaller suction catheter to reduce negative pressure and trauma"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Suctioning duration should be <10 seconds per pass. At 8 seconds with drop to 91% and tachycardia, this patient is hypoxemic from suctioning-induced airway obstruction. STOP immediately and allow recovery. Reoxygenate using the ventilator or manual ambu bag for 30–60 seconds, then resume suctioning only if necessary. Do NOT continue—hypoxemia worsens with each second. Pre-oxygenate before the NEXT pass (increase FiO2 to 100% or manually ventilate), then limit each pass to <10 seconds with post-suction reoxygenation. This patient may need to return to the OR for airway evaluation if suctioning is difficult.", "topic": "Suctioning Safety", "section": "skills", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient with a new tracheostomy is coughing forcefully, and the trach tube suddenly comes completely out of the stoma. The outer cannula is visible on the bed. Which is the correct sequence of actions?", "options": ["Reinsert the original trach tube immediately; reassess breathing after reinsertion", "Insert the obturator into the outer cannula; extend the neck; insert the outer cannula with obturator; remove obturator; assess lung sounds and O2 sat", "Call for help and retrieve the emergency trach kit; do not attempt reinsertion without assistance", "Assess the patient's breathing status first; if patient is compensating, wait for the provider to evaluate"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Accidental decannulation is a medical emergency. The sequence is critical: (1) Insert obturator into OUTER cannula only—it's a guide, never left in place. (2) Extend the neck and palpate/visualize the stoma. (3) Insert the outer cannula WITH obturator in a quick, smooth motion. (4) Immediately remove the obturator. (5) Assess lung sounds and O2 saturation. This sequence ensures a patent airway. Do not reinsert without the obturator (may cause tissue trauma and false passage). Do not delay—every second without an airway is critical. Assistance is good but should not delay reinsertion.", "topic": "Tracheostomy Skill", "section": "skills", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "You are instructing a patient on how to collect a sputum sample for culture and sensitivity. Which instruction is most important for obtaining a quality specimen?", "options": ["First thing in the morning, spit your saliva into the sterile cup and send it to the lab", "Rinse your mouth with water, then cough from deep in your lungs to bring up sputum, and collect it in the sterile cup", "Suck on a hard candy to stimulate secretions, then collect whatever mucus comes up", "Any time of day is fine; the key is collecting enough volume to fill the cup"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Sputum culture quality depends on specimen origin: must be from the LOWER lungs (where infection lives), not saliva (upper airway). Rinse mouth first to remove oral flora. Then cough deeply (from the chest, not the throat). Early morning is best—overnight secretion accumulation. Volume must be adequate but quality >> quantity. Lab won't process saliva mixed with sputum. C&S takes 48–72 hours, so getting it right the first time prevents delays in treatment. Hard candy is unnecessary and may contaminate the specimen.", "topic": "Specimen Collection Skill", "section": "skills", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient is scheduled for bronchoscopy with sedation at 8 AM. At 11 PM the night before, the patient eats a light dinner. You discover this after the fact. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": ["Notify the provider immediately; the procedure may need to be rescheduled due to aspiration risk", "Have the patient take laxatives overnight to clear the stomach", "Allow the procedure to proceed; the 9-hour fast is likely adequate", "Give activated charcoal to bind any food particles in the stomach"], "answer": 0, "rationale": "Bronchoscopy requires NPO 8–12 hours because sedation impairs protective reflexes (gag, cough, swallow). Aspiration of stomach contents into the lungs is life-threatening. Dinner at 11 PM before 8 AM procedure = 9 hours, which borders on adequate, but 'light' vs. 'full' meal matters. Standard practice: absolutely NPO after midnight (8 hours before 8 AM). Notify the provider—they decide whether to proceed, reschedule, or proceed with increased aspiration precautions (rapid-sequence intubation, etc.). Do NOT give laxatives or activated charcoal—these do not empty the stomach or prevent aspiration during sedation.", "topic": "Procedure Safety", "section": "skills", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "After a patient's bronchoscopy under conscious sedation, the provider has removed the scope and the patient is beginning to wake. The patient is drowsy but follows commands. Before allowing oral intake, which assessment is most critical?", "options": ["Patient is conscious; allow sips of water immediately to prevent dehydration", "Confirm the gag reflex has returned by asking the patient to swallow and observing for coughing", "Wait 30 minutes for full alertness before assessing gag reflex; sedation effects linger", "Check vital signs are stable; if they are, gag reflex assessment is unnecessary"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "The gag reflex is protective—it prevents aspiration. Sedation depresses this reflex. Before ANY oral intake (water, food, medications), confirm gag reflex has returned. Approach: touch the posterior pharynx gently with a tongue blade and observe for reflex contraction. If absent, wait and reassess. If present, start with small sips of water. Drowsiness does not equal gag reflex return—they are independent. Stable vitals confirm cardiovascular safety but NOT airway protection. Waiting 30 minutes is too long if reflex is already back (unnecessary NPO period). This is a critical safety check.", "topic": "Procedure Safety", "section": "skills", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient is being admitted with presumed pneumonia. You are to obtain a sputum specimen for culture and sensitivity. The patient's mouth is dry, and they say, 'I don't have anything to cough up right now.' Which intervention is most appropriate?", "options": ["Skip the sputum culture; proceed with other diagnostics and come back to it later", "Have the patient gargle with normal saline; encourage them to cough deeply", "Perform suctioning via nasotracheal approach if the patient cannot produce sputum", "Give the patient water to drink and wait 30 minutes for secretions to accumulate"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "If the patient cannot produce sputum immediately, facilitate: have them rinse with normal saline to moisten and stimulate cough. Encourage deep breathing and coughing (stand up if possible; bent-over position helps). If still unable to produce, nasotracheal suctioning is acceptable (though not ideal). Do NOT skip—C&S takes 48–72 hours; delaying collection delays diagnosis. Do NOT give water—it dilutes lower airway secretions and does not stimulate cough the way normal saline does. Timing: early morning after overnight secretion accumulation is ideal, but ill patients need specimens ASAP.", "topic": "Specimen Collection", "section": "specimen-communication", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A provider orders blood cultures for a febrile patient. The lab protocol requires two separate venipunctures from different sites (to differentiate contamination from true bacteremia). The patient, anxious about needles, asks, 'Can't you just draw both from my IV?' Which response is most appropriate?", "options": ["'Yes, I'll draw both from your IV to minimize needle sticks. The culture will still be accurate.'", "'No, we need two separate venipunctures from different sites. I'll use a small needle and make it quick.'", "'I can draw one from your IV and one from a venipuncture. That way, you only get one extra needle stick.'", "'Let me check with the lab to see if we can use your IV for both cultures.'"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Blood cultures from an IV contaminate easily (bacteria from the IV line, skin flora during insertion, etc.). Diagnosis of bacteremia requires two SEPARATE venipunctures from different body sites—this distinction rules out contamination. Drawing both from an IV defeats the purpose. Empathize with the patient's anxiety, explain WHY two sites matter (detecting true infection vs. contamination), and offer support (small needle, explain what you're doing, breathe together). One IV draw + one venipuncture is a compromise but not ideal. Do NOT check with lab; this is standard protocol. Therapeutic communication: educate the patient on the clinical reason, validate their feeling, then proceed.", "topic": "Therapeutic Communication", "section": "specimen-communication", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A hospitalized patient with an indwelling catheter needs a urine specimen for urinalysis and culture. The patient asks, 'Why can't you just use the urine in the bag? That's easier.' Which explanation best supports this teaching moment?", "options": ["'The urine in the bag has been sitting there for hours and is contaminated with bacteria from the tubing and bacteria that grew in the bag. We need a fresh specimen from the catheter port using sterile technique.'", "'The bag urine is fine for urinalysis, but we need a sterile specimen from a new catheter for the culture.'", "'The lab prefers fresh urine, but using the bag is acceptable if it looks clear and yellow.'", "'Actually, using the bag urine is fine; I'll just label it clearly for the lab.'"], "answer": 0, "rationale": "Catheter drainage bags are NOT sterile and should never be used for culture/analysis. Bacteria proliferate in the bag, urine sits for hours (allowing bacterial growth, cellular breakdown, and dilution), and tubing contaminates the specimen. Correct technique: use a sterile needle and syringe to aspirate urine from the catheter port (marked with a label), using aseptic technique. This is the ONLY appropriate method for catheterized specimens. Teaching the patient why matters—it builds trust and compliance when they understand the clinical reason. Do not label the specimen as 'sterile' if it came from the bag; this misleads the lab and can result in unnecessary antibiotics.", "topic": "Specimen Collection", "section": "specimen-communication", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient with a complicated post-op course is on multiple medications and has had a sudden drop in BP. The nurse needs to communicate this change to the surgical team. Which SBAR structure best conveys the critical information?", "options": ["S: 'BP dropped 20 points.' B: 'Post-op day 2 after major surgery.' A: 'Patient is probably in shock.' R: 'Give a blood transfusion.'", "S: 'Patient's BP is 88/52, down from 128/78 one hour ago; patient is alert.' B: 'Post-op day 2, on morphine q3h, furosemide this AM.' A: 'Volume loss (dehydration) or medication effect most likely.' R: 'Consider IV fluids or medication review.'", "S: 'Post-op day 2 patient has low BP.' B: 'Last assessment was normal.' A: 'Something is wrong.' R: 'Please come evaluate.'", "S: 'Everything is falling apart.' B: 'Patient was fine before.' A: 'Now patient is bad.' R: 'Do something urgently.'"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "SBAR: Situation (what's happening NOW—BP drop, current vitals, patient status), Background (relevant history—post-op day, recent meds, time of diuretic), Assessment (your clinical interpretation—likely hypovolemia or drug effect, not assumption of 'shock'), Recommendation (specific suggestion—fluids or med review). Option 1 over-interprets without data. Option 3 is vague and doesn't provide actionable info. Option 4 is emotional and unprofessional. Option 2 is organized, specific, and gives the provider the information needed to decide next steps. This communication style is what saves lives.", "topic": "Therapeutic Communication", "section": "specimen-communication", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient recently started on a new antihypertensive states, 'I stopped taking my pills because I felt fine and didn't think I needed them anymore.' Which response demonstrates both therapeutic communication and patient education?", "options": ["'That was a mistake. You must take them every day or your blood pressure will spike and you could have a stroke. Take it now.'", "'I understand you felt well—that's actually why medication for blood pressure is important. High BP usually has no symptoms, which is why it's called the 'silent killer.' Stopping suddenly can cause a dangerous spike. Let's talk about what made you stop and how we can help you stay on track.'", "'Did you stop because of side effects? If so, we can switch medications, but you need to check with your doctor first.'", "'It's fine to stop when you feel better, but you'll need to restart when the symptoms come back.'"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Therapeutic communication validates the patient's feeling (you felt well) while educating about the disease (asymptomatic nature of HTN). It avoids shame ('mistake') and commands ('must take'), instead inviting collaboration ('let's talk'). Explores barriers (side effects, understanding, motivation) without assuming. Option 4 is dangerous and medically incorrect. Option 3 assumes side effects without exploring. Option 1 shames and alienates the patient, reducing future compliance. Option 2 combines empathy, education, and problem-solving—proven to improve medication adherence. This is nursing advocacy.", "topic": "Therapeutic Communication", "section": "specimen-communication", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient on 4L nasal cannula has a pulse oximetry reading of 100% (SaO2). Arterial blood gas shows pH 7.48, PaCO2 32, PaO2 185. Which interpretation is most accurate?", "options": ["Oxygenation is optimal; continue current oxygen delivery", "The patient has mild respiratory alkalosis from hyperoxia and may be over-oxygenated; consider weaning oxygen", "These values indicate hypoventilation; increase oxygen delivery to increase CO2 elimination", "Pulse ox reading is inaccurate; obtain a new reading and repeat the ABG"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Pulse ox 100% + PaO2 185 = hyperoxia (goal typically 80–100 mmHg unless specific disease requires higher). pH 7.48 + PaCO2 32 = respiratory alkalosis (hyperventilation/low CO2). The patient does not need 4L—weaning is appropriate. Chronic hyperoxia can cause oxidative stress. In COPD, excessive oxygen can suppress respiratory drive, worsening CO2 retention. Goal: oxygen sat 95–99% on room air or minimal supplemental oxygen (unless pneumonia, PE, hypoxemic disease present). This patient is over-oxygenated. Wean to maintain sat 95–99%.", "topic": "Respiratory Labs/Diagnostics", "section": "respiratory-labs-diagnostics", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A sputum culture from a pneumonia patient grows Streptococcus pneumoniae. The sensitivity report shows: Penicillin S, Cephalosporin S, Macrolide R, Fluoroquinolone S. The patient was started on azithromycin (a macrolide) 48 hours ago. Current clinical status: persistent fever, cough, fatigue. Which action is most appropriate?", "options": ["Continue azithromycin; resistance takes longer to develop and the antibiotic may still work", "Notify the provider immediately; switch to an antibiotic the organism is susceptible to (penicillin, cephalosporin, or fluoroquinolone)", "Add a second antibiotic to overcome the resistance while continuing azithromycin", "Repeat the sputum culture to confirm the resistance result before changing antibiotics"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Macrolide Resistance (R) means azithromycin will NOT kill this organism. Continuing it risks treatment failure, persistent infection, and worsening clinical status. Notify the provider immediately. Switch to a susceptible antibiotic (penicillin, cephalosporin, or fluoroquinolone). Do NOT wait for a repeat culture—the susceptibility result is clear. Do NOT add another antibiotic without provider order. The patient's persistent fever after 48 hours on an ineffective antibiotic suggests treatment failure. Prompt switch to a susceptible agent is critical.", "topic": "Respiratory Labs/Diagnostics", "section": "respiratory-labs-diagnostics", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient presents with a chronic cough, hemoptysis, night sweats, and weight loss. TB skin test (Mantoux) is negative (induration 2 mm at 48 hours). Chest X-ray shows upper lobe infiltrate. Which test is necessary to establish a TB diagnosis?", "options": ["Repeat TB skin test in 1 week; negative skin test rules out TB", "Sputum AFB (acid-fast bacillus) smear and culture—this is the definitive diagnostic test for TB", "CT chest to better visualize the infiltrate and confirm tuberculosis", "Bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage to culture the organism"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "TB skin test can be falsely negative in immunocompromised patients (HIV, severe malnutrition) or early TB infection. A negative skin test does NOT rule out TB when clinical suspicion is high. Chest X-ray findings (upper lobe infiltrate) + classic symptoms (cough, hemoptysis, night sweats, weight loss) = TB until proven otherwise. Definitive diagnosis: sputum AFB (acid-fast bacillus) microscopy and culture. AFB shows the organism; culture confirms TB and allows drug susceptibility testing. Obtain three sputum specimens (morning samples preferred). CT is not diagnostic. Bronchoscopy is not first-line. Treatment may begin empirically while awaiting culture results.", "topic": "Respiratory Labs/Diagnostics", "section": "respiratory-labs-diagnostics", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient is scheduled for bronchoscopy. Past medical history includes chronic kidney disease (Cr 2.1), HTN, and a contrast allergy (rash with previous CT with iodinated contrast). Which contraindication or precaution applies?", "options": ["Contrast allergy is a contraindication to bronchoscopy; procedure cannot be performed", "CKD is a contraindication; IV contrast-enhanced imaging cannot be done safely", "No absolute contraindication to bronchoscopy itself, but if imaging with contrast is needed, use alternative (ultrasound) or premedicate for allergy", "Both contrast allergy and CKD absolutely contraindicate all diagnostic imaging"], "answer": 2, "rationale": "Bronchoscopy itself does not require IV contrast—it's direct visualization of the airways with a flexible scope. Contrast allergy does NOT contraindicate bronchoscopy. If imaging (CT) is needed post-procedure, use non-contrast CT, ultrasound, or premedicate the patient for allergy (steroids, antihistamines) if iodinated contrast is essential. CKD warrants BMP to assess kidney function, but bronchoscopy is safe (no IV contrast required). If contrast IS needed, use sparingly and flush with fluids afterward. Sedation precautions in CKD (reduced metabolism) but procedure is feasible. Allergy to 'iodine' or 'shellfish' is NOT a contraindication—only contrast media allergy.", "topic": "Respiratory Labs/Diagnostics", "section": "respiratory-labs-diagnostics", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A chest X-ray report states: 'Right basilar consolidation, no pleural effusion, heart size normal.' A second X-ray 3 days later shows: 'Right basilar and middle lobe consolidation, moderate right pleural effusion, heart size normal.' Which clinical interpretation is most accurate?", "options": ["Pneumonia is resolving; the patient should recover with continued antibiotics", "Pneumonia is worsening despite treatment; consider resistant organism or non-infectious cause", "Pleural effusion is a normal finding in pneumonia and indicates effective immune response", "Heart size is normal, so heart failure is not contributing to this patient's condition"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Progressive consolidation (basilar to basilar + middle lobe) + NEW pleural effusion over 3 days = worsening pneumonia. This suggests treatment failure: organism may be resistant, infection may be atypical (fungal, viral), or underlying condition (heart failure, malignancy, immunosuppression) may be complicating recovery. Repeating sputum culture, assessing antibiotic levels (if appropriate), and checking for resistant organisms is warranted. Some pneumonias develop parapneumonic effusions, but NEW effusion + spreading consolidation = problem. Normal heart size doesn't rule out cardiogenic causes (EF may be reduced despite normal size). Escalate care and investigation.", "topic": "Respiratory Labs/Diagnostics", "section": "respiratory-labs-diagnostics", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient with renal impairment (Cr 2.8) requires a high-resolution CT chest with iodinated IV contrast to evaluate for pulmonary embolism. What is the most appropriate pre-procedure nursing intervention?", "options": ["Obtain a BMP to assess BUN and creatinine; if Cr is elevated, hold the procedure pending provider review", "Administer normal saline IV before and after contrast; ensure adequate hydration; hold metformin if diabetic", "Cancel the procedure; contrast is absolutely contraindicated in renal failure", "Proceed without alteration; the risk-benefit is the provider's decision, not the nurse's responsibility"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Iodinated contrast is nephrotoxic and can precipitate acute kidney injury in patients with CKD. However, if the CT is essential (suspected PE is life-threatening), the provider must weigh risks vs. benefits. Nursing role: (1) Ensure BMP is current—Cr 2.8 is severely elevated. (2) Hydrate the patient: normal saline IV before and after contrast (counteracts nephrotoxic effect). (3) Hold metformin × 48 hrs post-contrast (lactic acidosis risk if acute renal injury occurs). (4) Monitor post-contrast Cr closely. Do NOT cancel—that's the provider's decision. Do NOT proceed without precautions. Communication with provider about renal status and precautions is essential. This is collaborative clinical judgment.", "topic": "Respiratory Labs/Diagnostics", "section": "respiratory-labs-diagnostics", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A thoracentesis is performed on a patient with a large pleural effusion. 1.2 L of clear, pale yellow fluid is removed. The patient reports immediate relief of dyspnea. Post-procedure chest X-ray shows a small pneumothorax. Vital signs are stable, oxygen sat 97% on room air. Which nursing action is most appropriate?", "options": ["Monitor closely for worsening respiratory status; provide supplemental oxygen if sat drops; notify provider of pneumothorax findings", "This is a complication; call for emergency transport to the ICU immediately", "Small pneumothorax is expected after thoracentesis; no intervention needed", "Have the provider place a chest tube emergently to re-expand the lung"], "answer": 0, "rationale": "Pneumothorax is a known complication of thoracentesis (needle may puncture the visceral pleura). Small pneumothorax (<2 cm at the hilum on CXR) in a hemodynamically stable, asymptomatic patient is often managed conservatively: close monitoring, supplemental O2 if sat drops, serial CXRs to ensure it doesn't enlarge. Notify the provider—they decide management (observation vs. chest tube). Dyspnea relief (from fluid removal) may mask early pneumothorax symptoms, so vigilance is key. Do NOT panic or emergently transfer unless respiratory status deteriorates. Do NOT place chest tube without provider order. This is a managed complication, not an emergency.", "topic": "Respiratory Labs/Diagnostics", "section": "respiratory-labs-diagnostics", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A TB skin test (Mantoux) is read at 72 hours post-injection. Induration is 8 mm. The patient is homeless, has no known HIV status, and was recently in a crowded shelter. Which interpretation is correct?", "options": ["8 mm induration is positive in this high-risk patient; further workup (CXR, sputum AFB) is indicated", "8 mm induration is borderline; repeat the skin test in 2 weeks", "8 mm induration is negative; TB infection is ruled out", "Interpretation depends on HIV status; obtain HIV test first"], "answer": 0, "rationale": "TB skin test interpretation is risk-stratified: ≥5 mm = positive in high-risk (homeless, shelter exposure, close TB contact, immunocompromised). ≥10 mm = positive in standard risk. ≥15 mm = positive in low-risk groups. This patient (homeless, shelter exposure) has high risk; 8 mm is POSITIVE. Next steps: chest X-ray (to look for active TB), sputum AFB (if infiltrate on CXR or symptoms), and consider IGRA (blood test) for confirmation. Do NOT repeat the test—induration has already formed. Do NOT wait for HIV status (obtained separately, but TB workup proceeds regardless). Latent TB infection requires treatment to prevent progression to active disease.", "topic": "Respiratory Labs/Diagnostics", "section": "respiratory-labs-diagnostics", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A lung biopsy is obtained via percutaneous needle under CT guidance for a suspicious nodule. Pathology is pending. The patient reports pain at the biopsy site and dyspnea. Chest X-ray shows a small pneumothorax (1.5 cm) and a small pulmonary hemorrhage. Vital signs: BP 132/78, HR 102, RR 24, SaO2 92% on 2L. Which nursing intervention is most important?", "options": ["Immediately call for emergency intubation; the patient is deteriorating", "Monitor vital signs and oxygen saturation closely; provide supplemental oxygen; notify provider; prepare for chest tube if pneumothorax enlarges", "The hemorrhage requires immediate surgical intervention; call OR to schedule emergently", "This is a normal complication of needle biopsy; discharge the patient home with follow-up instructions"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Small pneumothorax + mild hemorrhage are expected complications of lung biopsy. This patient is hemodynamically stable (BP normal, HR 102 is elevated but not shock). Sat 92% on 2L is acceptable; monitor closely. Dyspnea may improve with oxygen and rest. Management: close monitoring (serial vitals, CXRs), supplemental oxygen, pain control, notify provider. Chest tube is needed only if pneumothorax enlarges, respiratory distress worsens, or sat cannot be maintained. Hemorrhage from needle biopsy is usually self-limited. Do NOT emergently intubate a stable patient. Do NOT go to OR unless bleeding is massive (not described here). Follow-up CXR in 24 hrs to ensure no progression.", "topic": "Respiratory Labs/Diagnostics", "section": "respiratory-labs-diagnostics", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A PET scan for staging of suspected lung cancer shows increased uptake in the right lower lobe mass and also in the liver and bone. What is the clinical significance of these findings?", "options": ["The liver and bone uptake are artifacts; only the lung mass is significant", "PET scan findings suggest metastatic disease (spread to liver and bone), which affects cancer staging and treatment options", "Uptake in multiple sites indicates benign findings; malignancy is ruled out", "Only liver uptake is concerning; bone uptake is normal and expected"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "PET scan detects metabolic activity (glucose uptake). Increased uptake in lung, liver, AND bone suggests primary lung cancer with metastases—Stage IV (incurable by surgery alone). This drastically changes treatment approach: may shift from curative surgery to palliative chemotherapy/radiation. Prognosis is significantly worse than localized lung cancer. This finding is critical to communicate to the patient and oncology team. Uptake in multiple organs is NOT benign. Bone metastases carry pain and fracture risk. Liver metastases impair function. This patient needs systemic (not local) treatment.", "topic": "Respiratory Labs/Diagnostics", "section": "respiratory-labs-diagnostics", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient with active anterior epistaxis is instructed to pinch the nose and lean forward. After 10 minutes of continuous pinching, bleeding continues. Which action should the nurse take?", "options": ["Continue pinching for another 10 minutes; epistaxis often takes 20 minutes to stop", "Release the pinch to assess if bleeding has stopped, then resume if it continues", "Apply ice to the bridge of the nose and notify the provider; packing may be needed", "Tilt the patient's head back to prevent blood from dripping and swallowing to clear the airway"], "answer": 2, "rationale": "Direct anterior pinch below the hard bone for 10–15 minutes is first-line. If bleeding continues after 10–15 minutes of continuous pressure, notify the provider—nasal packing may be required. Do NOT release and recheck frequently (disrupts clot formation). Do NOT tilt head back (blood drains into airway; aspiration/swallowing risk). Leaning FORWARD allows drainage out the nose, not into the airway. Ice may help but won't stop active bleeding. Provider may need to pack the nose (using ribbon gauze soaked in epinephrine or thrombin), cauterize the vessel, or prescribe topical vasoconstrictor. Continuous bleeding beyond 15 minutes warrants intervention.", "topic": "Upper Respiratory", "section": "upper-respiratory", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient presents with severe epistaxis unresponsive to pinching for 20 minutes. BP is 168/92, HR 106. The bleeding appears to be coming from the posterior nasal cavity (blood pooling in the throat). Which is the most appropriate nursing action?", "options": ["Continue anterior pinch and reassess every 5 minutes", "Notify the provider immediately; posterior epistaxis requires specialist intervention and possible hospitalization", "Advise patient to use a nasal spray decongestant to reduce bleeding", "Have patient lie supine and swallow blood to clear the airway"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Posterior epistaxis (bleeding from deeper nasal vessels) is more serious than anterior. It does NOT respond to simple pinching and requires specialist intervention (ENT). Posterior packing (longer, more uncomfortable, requires hospitalization) or endoscopic cautery may be needed. Blood pooling in the throat = aspiration risk. Notify provider immediately. Do NOT continue simple pinching—time is wasted. Do NOT use nasal spray (ineffective for posterior bleeding). Do NOT lie supine (aspiration risk). Have patient sit forward, spit out blood, and prepare for specialist referral. This patient may need admission, airway monitoring, and possible transfusion if significant blood loss occurs.", "topic": "Upper Respiratory", "section": "upper-respiratory", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient presents with sore throat, fever (101.8°F), swollen tonsils with exudate, and swollen lymph nodes. Rapid strep test is positive for Group A Streptococcus. Which treatment approach is most appropriate?", "options": ["Supportive care only (fluids, rest, acetaminophen); most sore throats are viral and antibiotics are not indicated", "Prescribe antibiotics immediately (penicillin V or amoxicillin); Group A strep requires treatment to prevent complications", "Wait for throat culture results before starting antibiotics, even though rapid test is positive", "Treat with azithromycin (macrolide) as first-line antibiotic for strep throat"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Group A Streptococcus (GAS) pharyngitis is BACTERIAL and requires antibiotics. Rapid strep test is sensitive and specific (~95%); culture is not needed to confirm. Untreated GAS carries risk of acute rheumatic fever (permanent cardiac valve damage) and post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (acute kidney injury). Antibiotics PREVENT these complications—treatment is NOT optional. First-line: penicillin V or amoxicillin PO (10-day course). Azithromycin (macrolide) is second-line if penicillin-allergic. Do NOT wait for culture. Do NOT use supportive care alone for confirmed GAS. This is a preventive intervention against serious sequelae.", "topic": "Upper Respiratory", "section": "upper-respiratory", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient treated for bacterial pharyngitis with antibiotics reports persistent sore throat, difficulty swallowing (dysphagia), and fever (102.1°F) on day 5 of treatment. Examination reveals an asymmetrical bulge in the posterior pharynx on one side, pushing the uvula toward the opposite side. Which complication is most likely, and what is the priority action?", "options": ["The patient has developed a peritonsillar abscess; notify the provider immediately for possible drainage/I&D", "Treatment failure due to antibiotic resistance; switch to a different antibiotic class", "Viral infection superimposed on bacterial pharyngitis; add antivirals", "Normal post-infection swelling; continue antibiotics and reassure the patient"], "answer": 0, "rationale": "Peritonsillar abscess: asymmetric bulge, uvula deviation, dysphagia, persistent fever despite antibiotics. This is a serious complication of strep pharyngitis requiring urgent intervention (drainage/I&D by ENT). Risk of airway obstruction and spread of infection. Notify provider immediately. Do NOT wait for culture. Do NOT just switch antibiotics—drainage is needed. Airway management is critical (may need intubation if swelling worsens). Patient may need admission. IV antibiotics and pain control while awaiting drainage. This is NOT normal post-infection; bulging pharynx indicates localized pus collection.", "topic": "Upper Respiratory", "section": "upper-respiratory", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient with obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) is prescribed CPAP but reports, 'The mask is uncomfortable and I can't sleep with it on. I'm stopping it.' Vital signs and pulse ox show daytime somnolence (falling asleep during conversations) and sat 86% while sleeping without CPAP. Which nursing intervention is most important?", "options": ["Accept the patient's decision; CPAP compliance is optional and patient choice", "Educate on OSA complications (HTN, MI, stroke, sudden death); explore barriers to CPAP use; consider alternative devices (oral appliances, positional therapy)", "Increase oxygen without CPAP to improve sat during sleep", "Recommend weight loss as the only treatment needed; CPAP is a temporary measure"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "OSA with untreated apnea causes daytime somnolence (impairs function, safety), hypertension (long-term), increased MI/stroke risk, and sudden death during apneic episodes. Sat 86% during sleep = significant hypoxemia. CPAP compliance is THE most important intervention. Many patients struggle initially; barriers include discomfort, claustrophobia, partner issues. Nursing role: explore barriers, demonstrate proper mask fit, discuss gradual acclimation (use during TV/reading first), offer different mask styles (nasal pillows, oronasal, etc.). Alternative: oral appliance, positional therapy, or combination. Weight loss is beneficial but takes time; CPAP provides immediate symptom relief and prevents complications. This is NOT patient choice—untreated OSA is dangerous. Educate and problem-solve together.", "topic": "Upper Respiratory", "section": "upper-respiratory", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient is referred for a polysomnography sleep study to diagnose suspected OSA. Which statement best describes what the patient should expect during this test?", "options": ["A 30-minute office test where you wear a pulse ox monitor to detect oxygen drops during sleep", "An overnight sleep study in a lab where multiple electrodes and sensors monitor brain activity, eye movement, muscle activity, oxygen saturation, and breathing patterns", "A simple home test using a portable device to measure oxygen levels and heart rate", "A daytime test where you're observed while sleeping for 2–3 hours to assess for apnea episodes"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Polysomnography is the gold standard for OSA diagnosis. Full night in a sleep lab (or at home for portable studies): EEG (brain waves), EOG (eye movement), EMG (chin/leg muscles), ECG (heart), pulse ox (O2 sat), nasal airflow, chest/abdominal movement (respiratory effort), snoring microphone. Technician monitors all night and counts apneic episodes (pause ≥10 sec). AHI (Apnea-Hypopnea Index) = number of apneas + hypopneas per hour. AHI >5 = abnormal; >15 = moderate OSA. Data guides CPAP pressure prescription. Simple office tests or portable devices lack sensitivity. Daytime testing won't capture sleep apnea. Full polysomnography is essential for diagnosis and CPAP titration.", "topic": "Upper Respiratory", "section": "upper-respiratory", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient with a sudden onset of dyspnea, stridor, and drooling is brought to the ED. Epiglottitis is suspected. Which is the priority nursing action?", "options": ["Lay the patient flat and prepare for emergent intubation; do NOT examine the throat with a tongue blade", "Perform a gentle throat examination to visualize the epiglottis and confirm diagnosis", "Have the patient sit upright and lean forward; keep them calm; do NOT agitate; prepare for possible airway intervention", "Give oxygen via face mask and reassure the patient while awaiting provider evaluation"], "answer": 2, "rationale": "Epiglottitis is a medical emergency (airway can close suddenly). Stridor + drooling + dyspnea = impending airway obstruction. Priority: (1) Do NOT examine the throat or trigger gag reflex (may precipitate complete airway obstruction). (2) Keep patient upright/leaning forward (optimizes airway diameter). (3) Keep patient calm (agitation increases airway demand and can trigger obstruction). (4) Prepare for possible intubation at bedside with anesthesia ready. Do NOT lay patient flat (gravity worsens obstruction). This is 'let the patient assume their own position' situation. Immediate provider evaluation, possible throat culture/imaging, and antibiotics if epiglottitis is confirmed. Airway patency is fragile—avoid any trigger.", "topic": "Upper Respiratory", "section": "upper-respiratory", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient with a history of recurrent epistaxis is discharged from the ED after anterior packing is placed. The packing is to be removed in 3 days. Before discharge, which patient teaching is most important?", "options": ["Rest at home, use mouth breathing only, and avoid touching the packing", "The packing will likely come out on its own; if it does, reinsert it immediately", "Avoid blowing nose, straining, Valsalva, and contact sports; report bleeding, pain, or signs of infection to provider", "Take antibiotics only if fever develops; otherwise, no special precautions needed"], "answer": 2, "rationale": "Post-packing epistaxis management: (1) Avoid nose blowing, straining (Valsalva), coughing hard, heavy lifting—anything that increases nasal pressure. (2) Avoid contact sports. (3) Watch for signs of infection (fever, foul odor, purulent discharge from packing). (4) Monitor for rebleed (call if bright red blood drips). (5) Keep provider appointment for packing removal. Antibiotics are often prescribed prophylactically (bacteria can colonize packing). Mouth breathing helps. Do NOT reinsert packing if it falls out—call provider. The packing is uncomfortable but critical to hemostasis. Compliance with activity restriction prevents rebleeding when pack is removed.", "topic": "Upper Respiratory", "section": "upper-respiratory", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient recovers from acute pharyngitis. Two weeks later, they develop fever (101.5°F), joint pain (knees, elbows), and a new cardiac murmur. ESR and CRP are elevated. Which complication is most likely, and what is the significance?", "options": ["A new upper respiratory infection; treat with antibiotics", "Acute rheumatic fever (ARF), a sequela of untreated or inadequately treated Group A strep; can cause permanent cardiac valve damage", "Post-infectious reactive arthritis, which resolves on its own without intervention", "Endocarditis from bacteremia; blood cultures and echo are needed"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Acute rheumatic fever develops 2–3 weeks post-GAS pharyngitis, especially if untreated or undertreated. Presentation: fever, arthralgia/arthritis, cardiac involvement (murmur = valve inflammation), elevated inflammatory markers. This is a medical emergency. Strep infection triggered an autoimmune response damaging the heart valves (mitral regurgitation or stenosis—permanent). ARF can lead to chronic rheumatic heart disease requiring valve replacement. Treatment: anti-inflammatory therapy (high-dose aspirin, sometimes corticosteroids), prophylactic penicillin for years, and cardiac monitoring. This is WHY treating GAS pharyngitis immediately is critical. Inadequate antibiotic treatment (short course, non-compliance) increases ARF risk. Long-term cardiac sequelae are devastating.", "topic": "Upper Respiratory", "section": "upper-respiratory", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A 58-year-old with a 40 pack-year smoking history presents with persistent hoarseness for 8 weeks, dysphagia, and a palpable neck mass. Laryngoscopy reveals a laryngeal mass. Which staging investigation and patient education point are most critical?", "options": ["Biopsy the mass immediately; explain that it may be benign and surgery can be delayed", "Perform biopsy to confirm malignancy; explain that early-stage laryngeal cancer can have good cure rates with appropriate treatment (surgery or radiation)", "Order imaging (CT/MRI) only; avoid biopsy to prevent tumor spread", "Referral to oncology for chemotherapy as primary treatment without staging"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Laryngeal mass + hoarseness + dysphagia + neck mass = likely laryngeal cancer (80% of head/neck cancers linked to tobacco/alcohol). Biopsy confirms malignancy (pathology guides treatment). Early-stage laryngeal cancer (stage I–II) has 80–90% 5-year survival with surgery (laser/partial laryngectomy) or radiation. This is encouraging; many patients preserve voice. Later stages require multimodal treatment (chemotherapy + radiation or total laryngectomy). Biopsy does NOT cause spread (standard of care). Do NOT delay—hoarseness 6+ weeks warrants investigation. Education: tobacco cessation is critical (even post-treatment, continued smoking increases second malignancy risk). Imaging helps with staging and surgical planning. This is treatable if caught relatively early.", "topic": "Head/Neck Cancer", "section": "upper-respiratory", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient is scheduled for a total laryngectomy for advanced laryngeal cancer. Which preoperative teaching point is most important to address?", "options": ["'You will have a permanent tracheostomy and will no longer breathe through your nose or mouth. Your esophagus will be intact, so you can eat and drink after surgery once you heal.'", "'After surgery, you will need a permanent tracheostomy, will lose your natural voice, and will require speech rehabilitation.'", "'A laryngectomy is a minor procedure; you will be home in 2–3 days with full voice recovery.'", "'You will be able to speak normally with a speaking valve immediately after surgery.'"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Total laryngectomy = surgical removal of the larynx (voice box). Permanent tracheostomy is created. Breathing bypasses nose/mouth (trache is the new airway). Natural voice is lost (patient can learn esophageal speech, use electrolarynx, or get specialized voice prosthesis). Esophagus remains intact—the patient CAN eat and drink once healed (not during immediate post-op; usually tube-fed 10 days–2 weeks while swelling resolves). This is life-altering; emotional support and realistic expectations are critical. Do NOT minimize ('minor procedure'). Do NOT promise normal voice (impossible after laryngectomy). Do NOT say speaking valve will work (valve requires airflow from lungs through vocal cords—both are gone). Speech therapy and psychosocial support are essential.", "topic": "Head/Neck Cancer", "section": "head-neck-cancer", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient 3 weeks post-total laryngectomy is unable to produce effective esophageal speech and feels isolated. Current diet: tube feeding. Which interdisciplinary intervention is most important at this stage?", "options": ["Continue tube feeding indefinitely; esophageal speech will eventually return", "Refer to speech-language pathology and mental health; explore alternative communication methods (electrolarynx, voice prosthesis); coordinate swallow evaluation", "Discharge to home with instructions to practice esophageal speech alone", "'Focus only on swallow recovery; speech can wait until patient is emotionally ready'"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Post-laryngectomy: patient has lost their voice, facing permanent disability. Depression/isolation is common. Speech recovery is not automatic; it requires specialized therapy. At 3 weeks, swallow evaluation (modified barium swallow) is needed to assess readiness for oral intake. SLP teaches alternative speech methods: electrolarynx (handheld device—immediate, limited naturalness), esophageal speech (takes months to master, most natural-sounding but difficult), voice prosthesis (requires esophageal puncture—good middle ground). Mental health support addresses grief, adjustment, and coping. Occupational therapy for ADL adaptation. Coordinated team approach accelerates recovery. Tube feeding is temporary; oral diet usually begins when swallow is safe (often 2–3 weeks post-op, depending on type of closure). Do NOT isolate or delay speech therapy. Communication restoration is psychologically critical.", "topic": "Head/Neck Cancer", "section": "head-neck-cancer", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient 10 days post-total laryngectomy asks when they can start eating. Current NPO. Modified barium swallow (MBS) shows aspiration of thin liquids but safe swallow of puree. Which recommendation is most appropriate?", "options": ["'Start with clear liquids by mouth; you should be eating normally within a week'", "'Begin puree diet by mouth; thicken liquids to nectar consistency; advance diet as tolerated'", "'Continue tube feeding for another 2 weeks; you are not ready for oral intake'", "'Remove the tube feeding; start a regular diet immediately'"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "MBS results guide diet: safe swallow of puree = start puree (soft, moist foods). Thin liquids caused aspiration = thicken all liquids to nectar consistency (add thickener powder). Advance texture gradually (puree → ground → minced → regular) as swallow safety improves. Tube feeding can be reduced/discontinued once adequate oral intake is tolerated (typically 25–30 mL/meal advancing to full meals). Aspiration of thin liquids is high-risk (silent aspiration in laryngectomy patients without cough reflex). Do NOT progress too quickly to thin liquids. Continue tube feeding as supplement until oral intake is adequate. Monitor for signs of aspiration (fever, new cough, SOB, weight loss). Swallowing therapy improves function over weeks/months.", "topic": "Head/Neck Cancer", "section": "head-neck-cancer", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A laryngectomy patient is at risk for a mucus plug in the tracheostomy. Which nursing assessment finding is most concerning and requires urgent intervention?", "options": ["Secretions in the trach are thick and green; patient coughs occasionally", "Sudden severe dyspnea, inability to pass suction catheter, or change in vocal quality of trach sounds despite patient effort", "Patient reports mild discomfort at trach site; secretions are white and scanty", "Dressing around trach is moist; there is minimal bleeding from the stoma"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Mucus plug = potentially lethal complication in tracheostomy patients. Signs: sudden dyspnea (complete or near-complete obstruction), inability to pass suction catheter, silent trach (no air escape sounds). This is an emergency. Intervention: (1) Call for help. (2) Attempt to suction gently; if catheter meets resistance, do NOT force. (3) Try gentle saline lavage (nebulized saline or syringe irrigation). (4) If unable to clear, prepare for tube change or emergency airway management. Prevention is key: humidification, hydration, regular suctioning PRN. Option 1 (green secretions) warrants monitoring but is not emergency. Option 3 is normal post-op. Option 4 (moist dressing, minimal bleeding) is expected. A mucus plug can cause death in minutes—this is a recognized emergency in tracheostomy care.", "topic": "Head/Neck Cancer", "section": "head-neck-cancer", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A laryngectomy patient confides, 'I don't want to live like this. I feel disfigured and worthless.' What is the most therapeutic nursing response?", "options": ["'You will feel better in time. Many patients adjust and live full lives after laryngectomy.'", "'Don't say that. You should be grateful you survived cancer.'", "'I hear you're struggling with this major change. It's normal to feel this way. Let's talk to the social worker and mental health team about support. You're not alone in this.'", "'Focus on your recovery now; counseling can wait until you're stronger.'"], "answer": 2, "rationale": "Laryngectomy is emotionally traumatic: permanent tracheostomy (visible), loss of voice (identity), altered self-image, social isolation. Depression and suicidal ideation are common. Therapeutic response: (1) Validate feelings (do NOT minimize or dismiss). (2) Normalize reaction (this is a major loss). (3) Connect with professional support (social work, psychology, support groups for head/neck cancer survivors). (4) Reassure that adjustment is possible and life can be meaningful. Do NOT rush recovery or suggest patient 'should' feel differently. Do NOT delay mental health support—it is part of cancer care. Involve family/caregivers. Many laryngectomy patients go on to work, enjoy relationships, and find purpose; this takes time and support.", "topic": "Head/Neck Cancer", "section": "head-neck-cancer", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient with a large cavitary lesion in the right lower lobe is diagnosed with pulmonary tuberculosis (sputum AFB+). Which nursing precaution is most important for this patient?", "options": ["Standard precautions only; TB is transmitted through blood contact", "Airborne precautions with N95 respirator use; patient in negative-pressure isolation", "Contact precautions with gloves and gown for all interactions", "Droplet precautions with surgical mask; patient can be in a shared room"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "TB is transmitted via airborne droplet nuclei (inhaled during cough/sneeze). Cavitary TB is highly contagious. Nursing requirements: (1) Place patient in negative-pressure isolation room. (2) Use N95 respirator (standard mask insufficient) for all contact. (3) Continue until patient has taken TB meds for 2 weeks and clinical improvement shown (usually 3–4 weeks). (4) Sputum-smear positive patients are most infectious; daily sputum smears assess infectivity. (5) Educate patient on respiratory hygiene (cough etiquette). Standard and contact precautions are insufficient for TB. This patient should never be in a shared room while infectious. Healthcare worker protection is critical—TB is occupational hazard for staff.", "topic": "Lower Respiratory", "section": "lower-respiratory", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient with newly diagnosed TB is started on isoniazid (INH), rifampin (RIF), pyrazinamide (PZA), and ethambutol (EMB) as initial therapy. Which patient teaching point is most critical for medication adherence?", "options": ["'Take these for 2 weeks and stop; short courses are sufficient for TB.'", "'Take all four medicines for the full 6-month course (intensive phase 2 months, continuation phase 4 months) exactly as prescribed. Stopping early risks drug resistance.'", "'Take these medicines only when you feel sick; they're not needed when you're feeling well.'", "'You can stop once your sputum becomes negative; the infection is then cured.'"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "TB treatment requires ADHERENCE for 6 months minimum: intensive phase (2 months of INH/RIF/PZA/EMB) + continuation phase (4 months of INH/RIF). Stopping early risks incomplete cure and DRUG-RESISTANT TB (MDR-TB), which is harder to treat and requires 20+ months of therapy. Do NOT stop when feeling better—bacteria are still present but dormant. Sputum negativity (usually by 2–3 weeks) signals reduced infectivity, not cure. Many TB patients improve clinically before microbiologic cure. Non-adherence is a major cause of TB treatment failure and resistance. Strategies: directly observed therapy (DOT—healthcare worker watches patient take meds), pill organizers, phone reminders, support groups. This is PUBLIC HEALTH critical—untreated TB spreads in the community.", "topic": "Lower Respiratory", "section": "lower-respiratory", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient with TB develops peripheral neuropathy (numbness, tingling in feet) after 2 weeks on isoniazid. Which action is most appropriate?", "options": ["Continue INH; peripheral neuropathy is a minor side effect that will resolve", "Hold INH immediately and notify the provider; isoniazid-induced neuropathy requires INH discontinuation or pyridoxine supplementation", "Switch to a different first-line agent without consulting the provider", "Increase INH dose to speed TB cure and minimize neuropathy duration"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Isoniazid-induced peripheral neuropathy is dose-dependent and preventable with pyridoxine (vitamin B6) supplementation. Risk is higher in malnourished, diabetic, or HIV+ patients. Management: (1) Add pyridoxine 25–50 mg daily. (2) If neuropathy is severe, hold INH and notify provider—may need to restart after symptoms resolve. (3) If pyridoxine is not given, continue INH cautiously with close monitoring (neuropathy can progress to permanent nerve damage if untreated). Do NOT increase INH dose—this worsens neuropathy. Do NOT switch agents alone (provider decision; may require therapy adjustment). Pyridoxine supplementation is standard practice for all TB patients on INH to prevent this complication. This is a manageable side effect, not a reason to abandon TB treatment.", "topic": "Lower Respiratory", "section": "lower-respiratory", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient with TB is being discharged home on anti-TB therapy. Which key safety information is most important before discharge?", "options": ["TB is no longer contagious after 2 weeks of therapy; masking is no longer needed", "'You must take all medications as prescribed for the full 6-month course. Take these exact doses—never skip or double-dose. Report side effects immediately. Rifampin may turn body fluids (urine, sweat) orange—this is normal. Use reliable birth control; TB meds reduce oral contraceptive effectiveness.'", "'Since you'll be home, TB infection risk is over; no special precautions needed for family'", "'Alcohol is fine to drink with your TB medications; there are no interactions'"], "answer": 1, "rationale": "Discharge teaching must address: (1) ADHERENCE: full 6-month course exactly as prescribed. (2) Infectivity window: airborne precautions recommended for first 2 weeks of therapy (when sputum is smear-positive). (3) Side effects: report immediately (hepatotoxicity, neuropathy, visual changes with EMB). (4) Drug-drug interactions: RIF reduces warfarin/OCP effectiveness—use backup contraception. (5) Rifampin side effects: orange urine/body fluids are expected, not harmful. (6) Alcohol: increases hepatotoxicity risk with INH/RIF; limit or avoid. (7) Family/contacts: TB skin test exposure prophylaxis may be needed. (8) Follow-up: sputum smears at 2, 4, 6 weeks (assess treatment response); CXR at end of therapy. Non-adherence is the biggest threat to TB cure and public health.", "topic": "Lower Respiratory", "section": "lower-respiratory", "type": "multiple-choice"},
    {"stem": "A patient with TB and HIV co-infection is about to start both antiretroviral therapy (ART) and anti-TB therapy simultaneously. Which nursing consideration is most important?", "options": ["Start ART and TB meds at the same time to maximize treatment speed", "Delay ART until TB treatment is complete (minimum 6 months) to avoid drug interactions", "Start TB meds first for 2 weeks, then add ART to reduce immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS) risk", "TB treatment is contraindicated in HIV+; focus only on ART"], "answer": 2, "rationale": "TB/HIV co-infection requires careful sequencing. IRIS (Immune Reconstitution Inflammatory Syndrome) risk: rapid immune recovery (from ART) + persistent TB infection = exaggerated inflammatory response. Strategy: (1) Start TB meds first (usually 2 weeks before ART). (2) Then add ART when CD4 >50 cells/µL (higher CD4 at TB diagnosis may allow concurrent start with caution). (3) Monitor closely for IRIS symptoms (fever, respiratory distress, lymphadenitis, CNS involvement). (4) Some RIF-containing TB regimens reduce ART levels—coordinate with ID specialist. (5) Adherence to both regimens is critical. Do NOT delay ART (CD4 <200 = AIDS; opportunistic infections; death). Do NOT start simultaneously without specialist guidance (increased toxicity and IRIS risk). Timing is key. Close monitoring for side effects and drug interactions. This is complex—multidisciplinary approach essential.", "topic": "Lower Respiratory", "section": "lower-respiratory", "type": "multiple-choice"}
  ]
};
